@section('title')Nested section@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>A <code>@@section</code> is a named piece of a page, and the layout places it where it yields that name. It must sit at the top level of the view. A section inside an <code>@@if</code>, a loop, or another section is rejected, and rendering stops with an error.</p>
  <h2>What causes it</h2>
  <p>The view below puts a section inside a condition:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@if ($flag)
  @@section('content')
    This content is inside an if block.
  @@endsection
@@endif</code></pre>
  <h2>What you see</h2>
  <p>In a development build, the debug page names the section, the view, and the rule that was broken:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@section('content') in `errors.nested_section` must be at the top level of the template</code></pre>
  <figure class="my-6">
    <img src="{{ asset('resources/images/nested-section-debug.png') }}" alt="The debug error page for a section inside an if block" class="w-full rounded border border-hairline">
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-text">The debug page for this error, as it appears in a development build.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>This page is a debug-only demo, and its route is absent in a release build. A release build compiles only the views the app lists in <code>build.rs</code>, and this one is not listed, so the browser never sees this error in release. In release, a render error on a compiled view is a generic 500, and the details go to the log.</p>
  <h2>How to fix it</h2>
  <p>Keep the section at the top level, and move the condition inside it:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@section('content')
  @@if ($flag)
    This content is inside an if block.
  @@endif
@@endsection</code></pre>
  <p>Sections are how a layout gets its content, and the layouts page explains the rules in full: <a href="/docs/layouts">layouts and sections</a>.</p>
@endsection
